.Dd November 10, 2026
.Dt FILEOPS 9
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm fileops ,
.Nm fo_pathconf ,
.Nm fo_statfs ,
.Nm fo_fsync ,
.Nm fo_setattr ,
.Nm fo_fadvise ,
.Nm fo_readdir ,
.Nm fo_copy_file_range ,
.Nm fo_acl ,
.Nm fo_extattr ,
.Nm fo_fcntl ,
.Nm fo_advlock ,
.Nm getvnode_fp ,
.Nm vn_setattr_checked
.Nd operations on an open file descriptor
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/param.h
.In sys/file.h
.Ft int
.Fn fo_pathconf "struct file *fp" "int name" "long *valuep" "struct thread *td"
.Ft int
.Fn fo_statfs "struct file *fp" "struct statfs *buf" "struct thread *td"
.Ft int
.Fn fo_fsync "struct file *fp" "int mode" "struct thread *td"
.Ft int
.Fn fo_setattr "struct file *fp" "struct vattr *vap" "struct ucred *active_cred" "struct thread *td"
.Ft int
.Fn fo_fadvise "struct file *fp" "off_t offset" "off_t len" "int advice" "struct thread *td"
.Ft int
.Fn fo_readdir "struct file *fp" "struct uio *uio" "off_t *basep" "struct thread *td"
.Ft int
.Fn fo_copy_file_range "struct file *infp" "off_t *inoffp" "struct file *outfp" "off_t *outoffp" "size_t *lenp" "unsigned int flags" "struct thread *td"
.Ft int
.Fn fo_acl "struct file *fp" "int op" "int type" "struct acl *aclp" "struct thread *td"
.Ft int
.Fn fo_extattr "struct file *fp" "int op" "int attrnamespace" "const char *attrname" "void *data" "size_t nbytes" "struct uio *auio" "struct thread *td"
.Ft int
.Fn fo_fcntl "struct file *fp" "int cmd" "intptr_t arg" "struct thread *td"
.Ft int
.Fn fo_advlock "struct file *fp" "void *id" "int op" "struct flock *fl" "int flags" "struct thread *td"
.In sys/filedesc.h
.Ft int
.Fn getvnode_fp "struct file *fp" "bool path"
.In sys/vnode.h
.Ft int
.Fn vn_setattr_checked "struct file *fp" "struct vattr *vap" "struct ucred *active_cred"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Every open file
.Pq Vt "struct file"
carries a pointer,
.Va f_ops ,
to a
.Vt "struct fileops"
table that implements each operation a process can perform on the
descriptor.
System calls that take a file descriptor dispatch through this table and
never through the descriptor's type
.Pq Va f_type
or through a vnode the file may carry
.Pq Va f_vnode ;
where one must tell an
.Dv O_PATH
descriptor apart it uses
.Fn FILE_IS_OPATH ,
which compares the table itself
.Pq Va f_ops
with
.Va path_fileops
and, for a served file, which has a table of its own for
.Dv O_PATH ,
tests the open flag.
A descriptor type therefore answers for itself, and a type that is not
backed by a vnode is complete without one.
.Pp
A program must nevertheless see the errors it always saw, in the order it
always saw them.
Where a system call refused a descriptor before doing anything else
\(em because it carries no vnode, because it is an
.Dv O_PATH
descriptor, or because of its type \(em
it still makes that refusal first, and only a served file
.Pq Dv DTYPE_DQ ,
which carries no vnode and answers through its table, is let past it.
.Fn getvnode_fp
makes the two tests of
.Fn getvnode
on a file the caller already holds, in their order:
.Er EINVAL
for a file without a vnode, then
.Er EBADF
for an
.Dv O_PATH
file unless the caller accepts one, as
.Fn getvnode_path
did.
A served file is exempt from the first.
.Pp
The MAC entry points follow the same rule.
A descriptor that is not served gets exactly the checks it always got, in
the order it always got them: where
.Xr fchmod 2
and
.Xr fchown 2
made the vnode's check before the table was asked, they still do, so a
policy's errno comes before the table's refusal of an
.Dv O_PATH
descriptor or a pseudo-terminal master; no file-level check is added in
front of them.
A served file has no vnode to check and gets
.Fn mac_file_check_setattr
on every call that sets an attribute through its descriptor:
.Xr fchmod 2 ,
.Xr fchown 2 ,
.Xr fchflags 2 ,
.Xr ftruncate 2 ,
.Xr futimens 2 ,
.Xr futimes 2 ,
the Linux file attribute flags and a change through
.Pa /dev/fd/N .
.Pp
Each member has an inline dispatch function of the same name.
Members that a table leaves
.Dv NULL
dispatch to a type-neutral default, named
.Fn invfo_* ,
which returns the error the operation has always returned for a descriptor
of no particular type.
Vnode-backed descriptors use the
.Fn vn_*
implementations.
.Pp
A table also describes its type through
.Va fo_flags .
Besides
.Dv DFLAG_PASSABLE
and
.Dv DFLAG_SEEKABLE ,
.Dv DFLAG_PRIVWRITE
marks a type whose
.Dv MAP_PRIVATE
mappings are copy-on-write and never reach the object, so
.Xr mmap 2
grants
.Dv PROT_WRITE
on such a mapping of a descriptor that lacks the
.Dv CAP_MMAP_W
right.
Vnode files,
.Xr devfs 4
nodes and shm objects set it;
.Xr mmap 2
tests the flag, never the descriptor's type.
.Dv DFLAG_FLOCK
and
.Dv DFLAG_RECLOCK
mark the types that take
.Xr flock 2
locks and
.Xr fcntl 2
record locks:
.Xr flock 2
refuses a table without the first with
.Er EOPNOTSUPP ,
the record-lock commands a table without the second with
.Er EBADF ,
both before looking at their arguments, as the type tests did.
The FIFO table carries
.Dv DFLAG_FLOCK
only; an anonymous pipe shares that table, and
.Xr flock 2
refuses it by its type with the same
.Er EOPNOTSUPP .
.Pp
The vnode bodies of the per-open vnode tables run inside the file context
.Pq Fn vn_file_context_enter
like every other member of those tables, where the system calls they
replace entered none; filesystems that key on the open file, such as
.Xr fusefs 4 ,
and
.Xr inotify 2
therefore see the per-open identity for these operations too.
.Ss Descriptors that carry a vnode without being vnode files
Several descriptor types keep a reference to the vnode they were opened
through although they are not
.Dv DTYPE_VNODE
files served by
.Va vnops :
FIFOs
.Pq Va pipeops ,
pseudo-terminal masters,
.Xr devfs 4
nodes under their own tables, and linuxkpi files.
For every operation that is answered by the vnode, these tables install the
.Fn vn_*
implementation explicitly, so the descriptor answers exactly as its vnode
does; no default inspects
.Va f_vnode .
A table that does not carry a vnode must not install a
.Fn vn_*
member.
.Pp
Three tables carry a vnode only on some creation paths: a FIFO has one and
an anonymous pipe none
.Pq Va pipeops ;
a pseudo-terminal master has one when opened through
.Pa /dev/ptmx
and none from
.Xr posix_openpt 2 ;
a linuxkpi file has one when opened through
.Xr devfs 4
and none from
.Fn fd_install .
Those tables install the
.Fn vn_*_opt
variants, which answer through the vnode when it is present and give the
type-neutral answer otherwise, and keep a function of their own only where
they have their own answer
.Pq Fn pipe_pathconf , Fn pipe_advlock .
Installing a plain
.Fn vn_*
member there dereferences a null vnode.
.Ss fo_pathconf
.Fn fo_pathconf
answers
.Xr fpathconf 2 :
it stores the value of the configuration variable
.Fa name
through
.Fa valuep
and returns 0, or returns an error.
.Dv _PC_ASYNC_IO
is answered by the system call for every descriptor and never reaches the
table.
.Bl -tag -width "devfs nodes"
.It vnode files
.Fn vn_pathconf
locks the vnode shared and calls
.Xr VOP_PATHCONF 9 .
Directories, regular files,
.Dv O_PATH
files, FIFOs, pseudo-terminal masters, devfs nodes and linuxkpi files all
use it.
.It pipes and sockets
Answer
.Dv _PC_PIPE_BUF
with
.Dv PIPE_BUF
and return
.Er EINVAL
for every other name.
A FIFO is a pipe that carries a vnode and answers through the vnode
instead.
.It everything else
.Fn invfo_pathconf
returns
.Er EOPNOTSUPP .
.El
.Ss fo_statfs
.Fn fo_statfs
answers
.Xr fstatfs 2
by filling
.Fa buf .
.Bl -tag -width "devfs nodes"
.It vnode files
.Fn vn_statfs
references the vnode's mount, runs the MAC mount-stat check, and calls
.Fn kern_do_statfs ,
which busies the mount, calls
.Xr VFS_STATFS 9
and applies jail
.Xr statfs 2
enforcement.
All of it needs the mount, so none of it is left in the system call.
Directories, regular files,
.Dv O_PATH
files, FIFOs, pseudo-terminal masters, devfs nodes and linuxkpi files all
use it.
.It everything else
.Fn invfo_statfs
returns
.Er EINVAL ,
the error a descriptor without a vnode has always received.
.El
.Ss fo_fsync
.Fn fo_fsync
flushes the file according to
.Fa mode :
.Bl -tag -width "FO_FSYNC_FULL" -compact
.It Dv FO_FSYNC_FULL
data and metadata, as
.Xr fsync 2
and
.Dv LIO_SYNC ;
.It Dv FO_FSYNC_DATA
data only, as
.Xr fdatasync 2
and
.Dv LIO_DSYNC ;
.It Dv FO_FSYNC_FS
the whole filesystem the file is on, as Linux
.Fn syncfs .
.El
.Bl -tag -width "devfs nodes"
.It vnode files
.Fn vn_fsync
is the one implementation for the system calls,
.Xr aio_fsync 2
and
.Fn syncfs .
It suspends writes on the mount, locks the vnode, cleans its pages and
calls
.Xr VOP_FSYNC 9
or
.Xr VOP_FDATASYNC 9 ,
retrying on
.Er ERELOOKUP ;
for
.Dv FO_FSYNC_FS
it busies the mount and runs
.Xr VFS_SYNC 9 .
It enters the file context itself, so every table installs it directly.
Directories, regular files, FIFOs, pseudo-terminal masters, devfs nodes,
linuxkpi files and
.Dv O_PATH
files use it;
.Xr fsync 2
and
.Xr fdatasync 2
refuse a descriptor without a vnode with
.Er EINVAL
and then an
.Dv O_PATH
descriptor with
.Er EBADF
before reaching the table
.Pq Fn getvnode_fp ,
as they always did.
.Xr aio_fsync 2
makes the same two refusals when the request is queued, and Linux
.Fn syncfs
accepts an
.Dv O_PATH
descriptor, both as they always did.
.It everything else
.Fn invfo_fsync
returns
.Er EINVAL .
.El
.Ss fo_setattr
.Fn fo_setattr
sets the attributes given in
.Fa vap ,
the whole
.Vt "struct vattr" :
size, mode, owner, the access, modification and birth times and the file
flags; a field holding
.Dv VNOVAL
is left alone.
It answers
.Xr futimens 2
and
.Xr futimes 2
for every descriptor, and for a served file also
.Xr fchflags 2 ,
the Linux file attribute flags and the attribute changes made through
.Pa /dev/fd/N ,
which
.Xr fdescfs 4
forwards in one call.
For any other file those three keep the bodies they always had:
.Xr fchflags 2
and the Linux flags call
.Fn setfflags
on the vnode after the audit record and the checks they always made,
and
.Xr fdescfs 4
sets the whole
.Vt "struct vattr"
with one
.Xr VOP_SETATTR 9
through
.Fn vn_setattr_checked ,
so that a change is made in full or not at all.
Size, mode and owner have direct operations of their own,
.Fn fo_truncate ,
.Fn fo_chmod
and
.Fn fo_chown ,
and every
.Fn fo_setattr
body applies them through those, in that order, by calling
.Fn file_setattr_direct
first: the table's own answers stand, the
.Fn badfo_*
refusals of an
.Dv O_PATH
descriptor and the vnode bodies' MAC checks and audit records among them.
.Bl -tag -width "devfs nodes"
.It vnode files
.Fn vn_setattr
sets the times and flags after the direct operations have run, carrying
the checks the system calls used to make themselves: the vnode audit record, the
privilege to set flags on a device, the MAC flag and time checks and
notifications, and the birth-time adjustment of
.Xr utimes 2 .
It then suspends writes on the mount, locks the vnode and calls
.Xr VOP_SETATTR 9 .
Directories, regular files, FIFOs, pseudo-terminal masters, devfs nodes,
linuxkpi files and
.Dv O_PATH
files use it;
.Xr futimens 2 ,
.Xr futimes 2
and
.Xr fchflags 2
refuse a descriptor without a vnode with
.Er EINVAL
and then an
.Dv O_PATH
descriptor with
.Er EBADF
before reaching the table
.Pq Fn getvnode_fp ,
as they always did.
.It everything else
.Fn invfo_setattr
runs the direct operations and returns
.Er EINVAL
for times and flags, what
.Fn getvnode
returned.
A file without a vnode is not reached by
.Xr fdescfs 4
at all, unless it is a served file: it keeps the answer
.Fn getvnode
gave there, refusing
.Xr chflags 2
with
.Er EOPNOTSUPP
and silently dropping every other change.
.El
.Ss fo_fadvise
.Fn fo_fadvise
applies a
.Xr posix_fadvise 2
hint to the byte range
.Fa offset
and
.Fa len .
The system call validates the range and the advice, records the audit
argument, and refuses a descriptor without
.Dv DFLAG_SEEKABLE
with
.Er ESPIPE
before the table is consulted.
.Bl -tag -width "devfs nodes"
.It vnode files
.Fn vn_fadvise
keeps the sequential, random and no-reuse regions in the file's
.Va f_advice ,
which
.Fn vn_read
and
.Fn vn_write
consult, and passes the will-need and do-not-need hints to
.Xr VOP_ADVISE 9 .
Only a regular file has anything to advise about; directories and devices
return
.Er ENODEV .
.It everything else
.Fn invfo_fadvise
returns
.Er ENODEV .
.El
.Ss fo_readdir
.Fn fo_readdir
answers
.Xr getdirentries 2 :
it reads directory entries into
.Fa uio
from
.Va uio_offset ,
leaves
.Va uio_offset
at the next entry, and stores the offset the read began from through
.Fa basep .
The system call refuses a descriptor without a vnode with
.Er EINVAL
.Pq Fn getvnode_fp ,
then
.Dv O_PATH
descriptors and files not opened for reading with
.Er EBADF ,
as it always did, and holds the file's offset lock around the call.
.Bl -tag -width "devfs nodes"
.It vnode files
.Fn vn_readdir
locks the vnode shared, returns
.Er ENOTDIR
for anything that is not a directory, runs the MAC readdir check and
.Xr VOP_READDIR 9 ,
and, when a union mount's root directory is exhausted, moves the file to
the covered directory and reads on, as it always did.
Directories use it; FIFOs, devfs nodes and vnode-carrying pseudo-terminal
masters and linuxkpi files reach it and get
.Er ENOTDIR .
.It everything else
.Fn invfo_readdir
returns
.Er EINVAL .
.El
.Ss fo_copy_file_range
.Fn fo_copy_file_range
answers
.Xr copy_file_range 2 ,
dispatched on the input file's table.
.Fa lenp
carries the requested length in and the copied length out; the offsets
are updated whatever the result.
The system call validates the flags, clamps the length, takes both files'
offset locks, refuses a file without a vnode with
.Er EINVAL
as it always did, checks the open modes, returns 0 for a zero length and
refuses negative offsets; then the input descriptor's table answers.
.Bl -tag -width "vnode files"
.It vnode files
.Fn vn_copy_range
refuses overlapping ranges of one file, range-locks both vnodes, and runs
.Fn vn_copy_file_range
inside the file context.
.Va vnops
and the per-open vnode tables install it: FIFOs, devices and
pseudo-terminal masters were refused by
.Fn vn_copy_file_range
and keep the same
.Er EINVAL
through the default.
.It served files
.Pq Dv DTYPE_DQ
install
.Fn file_generic_copy_range ,
which
.Fn vn_copy_range
also calls when the output is served.
It applies the checks of
.Fn vn_copy_range
and
.Fn vn_copy_file_range
in their order, on a vnode file's type and attributes and on a served
file's
.Fn fo_stat
answer: overlapping ranges of one identity
.Pq Va st_dev , st_ino
are
.Er EINVAL ,
a directory
.Er EISDIR ,
any other file that is not regular
.Er EINVAL ,
and
.Dv COPY_FILE_RANGE_CLONE
.Er EOPNOTSUPP .
Before the first read it clips the count at the output's
.Dv RLIMIT_FSIZE ,
as
.Fn vn_generic_copy_file_range
does; an output offset at or past the limit is
.Er EFBIG
with
.Dv SIGXFSZ
and reads nothing.
It then copies with
.Fn fo_read
and
.Fn fo_write
at explicit offsets, one buffer at a time: a short read is the end of the
input, a short write ends the copy, a write that fails counts none of its
buffer, and a signal after some bytes were copied ends it with the count
copied.
It does not look for holes: zeros in the input are written as data, so
the output gets no holes where a vnode copy may leave them, and the output
is never truncated, nor extended past the last byte written.
Unlike
.Fn vn_copy_range ,
no range lock covers both files for the whole copy: each read and each
write holds its own file's range lock.
.It everything else
.Fn invfo_copy_file_range
returns
.Er EINVAL .
.El
.Ss fo_acl
.Fn fo_acl
answers the
.Fn __acl_get_fd ,
.Fn __acl_set_fd ,
.Fn __acl_delete_fd
and
.Fn __acl_aclcheck_fd
system calls, selected by
.Fa op
.Pq Dv FO_ACL_GET , Dv FO_ACL_SET , Dv FO_ACL_DELETE , Dv FO_ACL_CHECK ,
for the
.Vt acl_type_t
.Fa type .
The system calls record the descriptor audit argument and refuse set and
delete on a descriptor without a vnode with
.Er EINVAL
and then on an
.Dv O_PATH
descriptor with
.Er EBADF ,
as they always did; get and check accept one.
.Bl -tag -width "devfs nodes"
.It vnode files
.Fn vn_acl
calls the
.Fn vacl_*
helper for the operation, each of which locks the vnode and runs its own
MAC check and audit record before
.Xr VOP_GETACL 9 ,
.Xr VOP_SETACL 9
or
.Xr VOP_ACLCHECK 9 .
Regular files, directories,
.Dv O_PATH
files, FIFOs, devfs nodes and vnode-carrying pseudo-terminal masters and
linuxkpi files use it, and get whatever their filesystem answers.
.It everything else
.Fn invfo_acl
returns
.Er EINVAL .
.El
.Ss fo_extattr
.Fn fo_extattr
answers the
.Xr extattr_set_fd 2 ,
.Xr extattr_get_fd 2 ,
.Xr extattr_delete_fd 2
and
.Xr extattr_list_fd 2
system calls and the Linux
.Fn f*xattr
family, selected by
.Fa op
.Pq Dv FO_EXTATTR_SET , Dv FO_EXTATTR_GET , Dv FO_EXTATTR_DELETE , Dv FO_EXTATTR_LIST .
Set and get take the user buffer in
.Fa data
and
.Fa nbytes
.Pq get with a null buffer returns the size ,
list fills
.Fa auio ,
delete takes neither.
The system calls record the audit arguments and accept
.Dv O_PATH
descriptors, as they always did.
.Bl -tag -width "devfs nodes"
.It vnode files
.Fn vn_extattr
calls the
.Fn extattr_*_vp
helper for the operation, each of which locks the vnode and runs its own
MAC check before
.Xr VOP_SETEXTATTR 9 ,
.Xr VOP_GETEXTATTR 9 ,
.Fn VOP_DELETEEXTATTR
or
.Xr VOP_LISTEXTATTR 9 .
Regular files, directories,
.Dv O_PATH
files, FIFOs, devfs nodes and vnode-carrying pseudo-terminal masters and
linuxkpi files use it, and get whatever their filesystem answers.
.It everything else
.Fn invfo_extattr
returns
.Er EINVAL .
.El
.Ss fo_fcntl
.Fn fo_fcntl
answers the
.Xr fcntl 2
commands whose meaning depends on the descriptor type and that the generic
file layer cannot answer itself:
.Dv F_READAHEAD
.Po
.Dv F_RDAHEAD
arrives as
.Dv F_READAHEAD
with the byte count already in
.Fa arg
.Pc
and
.Dv F_ISUNIONSTACK
from
.Xr fcntl 2 ,
and the kernel-private
.Dv F_GET_RW_HINT
and
.Dv F_SET_RW_HINT
.Pq Xr fcntl 2 commands that only the kernel issues
behind the Linux read/write life-time hints.
A command that yields a value stores it in
.Va td_retval[0] ,
as
.Fn kern_fcntl
does.
.Xr fcntl 2
refuses read-ahead on an
.Dv O_PATH
descriptor with
.Er EBADF
before the table is consulted, as it always did.
.Bl -tag -width "devfs nodes"
.It vnode files
.Fn vn_fcntl
sets the file's sequential read-ahead on a regular file
.Pq Er ENOTTY otherwise ,
reports whether the mount is a union stack, reads the vnode's write hint,
and sets it after a
.Dv VADMIN
access check.
Regular files, directories,
.Dv O_PATH
files and devfs nodes use it.
FIFOs, pseudo-terminal masters and linuxkpi files do not: the type tests
these commands used to make returned
.Er EBADF
for them, and the default keeps doing so.
.It everything else
.Fn invfo_fcntl
returns
.Er EBADF .
.El
.Ss fo_advlock
.Fn fo_advlock
answers
.Xr flock 2
.Pq Dv F_FLOCK in Fa flags , the file as Fa id ,
the
.Xr fcntl 2
record locks
.Po
.Dv F_POSIX
with the process leader as
.Fa id ,
.Dv F_OFD
with the file
.Pc
and the unlock of all POSIX locks that every
.Xr close 2
owes the process, with
.Fa op
one of
.Dv F_SETLK , F_GETLK , F_UNLCK
or
.Dv F_UNLCKSYS .
.Xr fcntl 2
rejects a lock request for a mode the descriptor was not opened with before
the table is consulted; whether the table takes locks at all is the table's
business.
The close-time unlock skips a table that has no
.Fn fo_advlock
member; the record-lock commands refuse a table without
.Dv DFLAG_RECLOCK
with
.Er EBADF
and
.Xr flock 2
a table without
.Dv DFLAG_FLOCK
with
.Er EOPNOTSUPP ,
both before looking at their arguments, as the type tests did.
For an
.Dv F_OFD
request
.Xr fcntl 2
also checks, before the request is looked at any further, that the
owner field is zero
.Pq Er EINVAL
and that the vnode's filesystem declares
.Dv VFCF_OFDLOCKS
.Pq Er EOPNOTSUPP ,
as it always did; a served file has no filesystem to ask and its table
answers.
.Dv FHASLOCK
is set ahead of an
.Xr flock 2
request and stays set whatever the table answers, as it always did; the
unlock at close is harmless when no lock was taken.
.Pp
The close-time unlock carries the kernel-private flag
.Dv F_CLOSEUNLCK
in
.Fa flags
beside
.Dv F_POSIX ,
with
.Fa op
.Dv F_UNLCK
and the whole file as the range:
.Fn closef ,
.Fn fdclearlocks
and the
.Xr fcntl 2
close-race recheck pass it, a user's
.Dv F_UNLCK
never does.
A table whose lock state lives outside the kernel (a served file, whose
locks the controller holds) uses it to release every POSIX lock of the
owner without waiting for an answer, and only when it ever took a lock for
that owner kind; a table that keeps its locks in
.Xr lockf 9
ignores it, and
.Fn vn_advlock
strips it before
.Xr VOP_ADVLOCK 9 .
.Bl -tag -width "devfs nodes"
.It vnode files
.Fn vn_advlock
calls
.Xr VOP_ADVLOCK 9
inside the file context.
Regular files, directories and devfs nodes use it.
A FIFO installs
.Fn pipe_advlock ,
which takes
.Dv F_FLOCK
requests to the vnode and refuses the rest as a pipe does.
.It everything else
.Fn invfo_advlock
returns
.Er EBADF ,
what the type tests gave the record locks and the close-time unlock of a
descriptor that takes no locks;
.Xr flock 2
never reaches it, having refused a table without
.Dv DFLAG_FLOCK
first.
An anonymous pipe shares the FIFO table and is refused by
.Xr flock 2
itself, by its type, with
.Er EOPNOTSUPP .
Pseudo-terminal masters and linuxkpi files do not install the member: the
type tests these system calls used to make refused them, and they stay
refused.
An
.Dv O_PATH
file installs one that answers only the close-time
.Dv F_UNLCK
of POSIX locks, because closing any descriptor of a file releases the
process's record locks on it; every other request gets the default.
.El
.Sh DTRACE
.Bl -tag -width "fd:::refused"
.It fd:::invfo
A default answered for a descriptor whose table has no member:
.Fa arg0
is the member's name,
.Fa arg1
the
.Vt "struct file *" ,
.Fa arg2
the errno.
The first probe to enable when a program gets
.Er EINVAL
from an operation on an unusual descriptor.
.It fd:::refused
A system call refused a descriptor before looking at its arguments:
.Fa arg0
the system call's name,
.Fa arg1
the
.Vt "struct file *" ,
.Fa arg2
the
.Dv DFLAG_*
bit the table lacks, or 0 for a descriptor without a vnode or an
.Dv O_PATH
descriptor,
.Fa arg3
the errno.
.El
.Pp
The
.Nm fd
provider also carries
.Nm advlock-start
and
.Nm advlock-done
around every advisory lock request, and every
.Fn mac_file_check_*
hook fires the
.Nm mac_framework
provider's probes.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
Each operation returns 0 on success or an
.Xr errno 2
value.
